<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pathet Lao</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1950">1950</a>–<a href="/years/1975">1975</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pathet Lao was the communist movement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>, founded in 1950 under Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/souphanouvong">Souphanouvong</a> with the support of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a>. It fought the Royal Lao Government in a long civil war that became part of the wider <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a> conflict. In 1975 it took power, abolished the monarchy and founded the Lao People's Democratic Republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pathet-lao.jpg" alt="Pathet Lao" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Laos.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Pathet Lao, Lao for Lao Nation, was officially the Lao People's Liberation Army. It traces its roots to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and to the Lao Issara, an anti-French, non-communist nationalist movement formed on 12 October 1945. It was renamed the Pathet Lao in 1950, when it was adopted by Lao forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/souphanouvong">Souphanouvong</a>, who joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a>'s revolt against French colonial authorities in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-indochina-war">First Indochina War</a>. In August 1950 he joined the Viet Minh at their headquarters north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> and became head of the Pathet Lao, with a political arm called Neo Lao Issara, the Free Lao Front. The movement was dependent on the Vietnamese communists from its foundation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1953 Pathet Lao fighters accompanied a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and set up a government at Viengxay in Houaphanh province. The Geneva Conference of 1954 required foreign forces to withdraw and allowed the Pathet Lao to establish itself in Laos's two northern provinces, though the Viet Minh and North Vietnamese never fully left the border areas. A coalition government of monarchists and communists was formed in 1957, but in 1959 fighting resumed after two Pathet Lao battalions chosen for integration into the Royal Lao Army were surrounded and fled to North Vietnam.</p><p>North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> formed Group 959 in September 1959 to secure the supply route to South Vietnam, later known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a> trail, and to train and supply the Pathet Lao.</p><p>A neutrality agreement was signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> on 23 July 1962 and the Pathet Lao joined another coalition government in June 1962, leaving it and resuming the fighting by April 1963. The armed forces took the name Lao People's Liberation Army in October 1965, with estimated strength rising from 25,000 in June 1965 to over 48,000 in 1970, and depended on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-army-of-vietnam">People's Army of Vietnam</a> for weapons. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> also supplied guns, grenades and bullets and trained over 700 officers. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-accords">Paris Peace Accords</a> the Pathet Lao and the Lao government signed the Vientiane Treaty, a cease-fire, on 21 February 1973. The movement gained control of the whole country in 1975.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Pathet Lao's supreme headquarters lay in the Viengxay Caves near Xam Neua. Its forces were divided into regular, regional and militia or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> units, with Vietnamese advisers attached, and its units had political commissars down to company level. Training stressed political indoctrination above military skill, and recruits came by appeals to patriotism or by a draft.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pathet Lao” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>North Vietnam also sent troops to fight beside the <b>Pathet Lao</b> in the Laotian Civil War and the Khmer Rouge in the Cambodian Civil War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/democratic-republic-of-vietnam">Democratic Republic of Vietnam · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The communist <b>Pathet Lao</b>, backed by North Vietnam, controlled the east, through which the Ho Chi Minh Trail ran, and the United States waged a massive secret bombing campaign.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-laos">Kingdom of Laos</a></li><li><blockquote>It dissolved in 1949, with Souvanna Phouma returning to the Kingdom of Laos and Souphanouvong founding the communist <b>Pathet Lao</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/lao-issara">Lao Issara</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Pathet Lao</b> gained control of the coalition government formed under the 1973 accords, King Savang Vatthana abdicated, and on 2 December 1975 the Lao People's Democratic Republic was proclaimed in Vientiane, with Prince Souphanouvong as president and Kaysone Phomvihane as prime minister.</blockquote><a href="/state/lao-peoples-democratic-republic">Lao People's Democratic Republic</a></li></ul></section>
